<ArticleLayout slug="product-photography-cost-per-sku" ctaLabel="Get a market range for your catalog" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Every page answering this question quotes a day rate, which tells you what a photographer costs and nothing about what your catalog costs. The number you need is per image, then multiplied by a figure only you know.</p>

  <h2>How much does product photography cost per SKU?</h2>

  <p>Published studio cards put standard ecommerce capture between roughly $39 and $50 an image in September 2026. Per SKU, multiply that by however many images each of your SKUs actually needs.</p>

  <p>That second number has no market answer. Nobody publishes an average number of images per SKU, because it is a decision about your catalog rather than a fact about the market: a t-shirt in four colorways is not a watch. So this page prices the image, publishes the volume curve, and leaves that multiplier to you rather than inventing one.</p>

  <h2>What published cards actually charge</h2>

  <p>Read one card at a time, not aggregated.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published studio rate cards put standard ecommerce capture between roughly $39 and $50 an image, with complexity uplifts on one card to $75 for ghost mannequin or oversized work, $100 for jewelry and watches, $150 for a hand model image and $190 for a full-body model image.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published product photography pricing pages in this category, read directly on 10 September 2026, two independent cards, prices quoted against the unit each card states.</p>

  <p>Note the spread inside one card. A jewelry image costs twice a plain product image and a full-body model image costs nearly four times it, so a catalog mix moves your average more than any discount you negotiate.</p>

  <p>The same shape appears in a second market, which is worth knowing before anyone tells you offshore capture is a different economics.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A published Chinese ecommerce photography rate card, quoting its own executing rates for the Guangzhou and Pearl River Delta region, prices flat lay at 80 to 150 RMB an image, hanging shots at 100 to 180, in-studio model work at 200 to 300, scene images at 150 to 300 and retouch at 50 to 200, with a five-style minimum and 6 to 10 images per style.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published Chinese ecommerce photography rate card, 20 July 2026, the publisher's own executing rates for one region, read in Chinese.</p>

  <p>Different currency, same structure: flat lay cheapest, model work several times more, retouch billed separately. The card also states a minimum, which is the first hint that small runs are priced differently from large ones.</p>

  <h2>Does cost per SKU fall at volume?</h2>

  <p>Yes, and then it stops, and where it stops is the useful part.</p>

  <p>The discount in this category is usually not a lower list price. It is sold as a membership you buy before you get the rate.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The published volume discount in this category is sold as a membership, not as a lower list price. One card publishes annual memberships at $480, $2,340 and $7,500 a year buying 10, 20 and 30 percent off product work.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published product photography pricing page in this category, read directly on 10 September 2026. The ladder appears on a single card.</p>

  <p>A membership is a bet on your own volume, so the first question is where it pays for itself. Divide each fee by what the discount saves on one image and the answer falls out.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Dividing each annual membership fee by the money it saves on one standard image gives the breakeven: 96 images a year on the 10 percent tier, 234 on the 20 percent tier and 500 on the 30 percent tier.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on a published pricing page in this category, read 10 September 2026. Fee divided by percentage saving on the card's own $50 standard image, no other input.</p>

  <p>Under 96 images a year, the cheapest tier costs you money. That is a straightforward test you can run against last year's actuals before anyone quotes you.</p>

  <p>Now the ceiling, which is the finding.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The published discount ladder stops at 30 percent off list. Past roughly 500 images a year, buying more photography on a published card does not make photography meaningfully cheaper.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published product photography pricing pages in this category, read 10 September 2026. The deepest published tier on any card read was 30 percent.</p>

  <p>And it is not a quirk of one market.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In both markets read for this piece, the published volume discount tops out at about 30 percent off list.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing surfaces in this category in two markets, September 2026. Two independent ladders that agree, not a measured market average.</p>

  <p>Two independent ladders, two currencies, the same ceiling. Whatever your procurement team believes about leverage at scale, the published cards say the leverage runs out at 500 images a year.</p>

  <h2>The curve, with the arithmetic shown</h2>

  <p>Carrying that ladder across volumes gives the actual curve.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Carried across the published ladder, effective capture falls from $50 an image at list to about $46.60 at 300 images, $41.25 at 1,200, $37.50 at 3,000, $35.63 at 12,000 and $35.25 at 30,000. A tenfold rise in volume above 3,000 images moves the unit price about 6 percent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on a published pricing page in this category, read 10 September 2026. List price times the card's own discount, plus the annual fee, divided by images. One card, one currency, no market average implied.</p>

  <p>To turn that into per-SKU numbers you need images per SKU, and since no source publishes an average, the table below uses six. Six is not a benchmark. It is a legible number that makes the arithmetic checkable, and you should rerun it with your own.</p>

