  <p>Read the last two rows together. Going from 2,000 SKUs to 5,000, more than doubling the catalog, saves about two dollars a SKU. Everything worth winning on this curve is won before you reach 500 SKUs.</p>

  <p>That is the sentence the day-rate pages never print, and it changes what you should be negotiating. Above the ceiling, arguing about the rate is close to pointless. What is left to move is the number of images and the share of them that need a camera at all.</p>

  <h2>What does a photography day rate not cover?</h2>

  <p>Fixed costs, mostly, and they behave very differently at small and large runs.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A separate published card charges $39 a photo plus a fixed $149 studio fee per booking. That fixed fee is about 62 percent of the unit price when six images come out of one booking, $63.83 an image, and under 1 percent at six hundred images, $39.25 an image. The list price never moved.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published product photography pricing page in this category, read 10 September 2026, with the fee amortized arithmetically across three run sizes.</p>

  <p>The list price never moved. That is worth pausing on: two buyers quoted the same $39 pay $63.83 and $39.25 for the identical image, purely on how many images came out of one booking. Batching is a bigger lever than negotiating here, and it costs nothing to pull.</p>

  <p>Credit blocks work the same way in a different wrapper.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Credit blocks on one published card run $1,500 for 25 credits, $2,500 for 50 and $4,500 for 100, which is $60, $50 and $45 a credit. A fourfold larger block buys 25 percent off the unit.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published product photography pricing page in this category, read 10 September 2026, block price divided by block size.</p>

  <p>Then there is what appears on no card at all.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No card read for this piece prices sample logistics, reshoots or approval rounds. Eight published price surfaces across two markets, and getting the product to the studio, getting it back, and shooting it again after a failed approval appear on none of them.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, two markets, read directly in September 2026. Absence confirmed by reading each card in full.</p>

  <p>Eight surfaces, two markets, and not one prices the three things most likely to blow a catalog schedule. Sample movement in particular is the line that decides whether a 2,000-SKU shoot takes six weeks or four months, and it is invisible in every quote you will compare.</p>

  <p>The useful way to hold all of this is as a fixed and variable split, because the two behave in opposite directions. The per-image rate is variable and barely moves once you clear the ladder. The booking fee, the platform fee and the membership are fixed, and their weight per image collapses as the run grows. Sample logistics and reshoots sit outside both, scaling with how many separate sessions you run rather than with how many images come out of them.</p>

  <p>Which points at a scheduling conclusion the rate sheet cannot give you. Fewer, larger sessions beat more, smaller ones at every volume, and the saving from consolidating bookings is usually larger than the saving from negotiating the rate.</p>

