  <h2>How much does ecommerce retouching cost per image?</h2>

  <p>Far less than capture, and confusing the two is how buyers end up comparing quotes that are not comparable.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published ecommerce retouch cards put the floor between roughly $0.25 and $1.20 an image by operation, with one card adding a fixed platform fee of about $95 a month on top.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, September 2026, two independent cards read directly and compared, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Put that against capture and the gap is not a negotiating range.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Against published capture of $39 to $50 an image, a per-image retouch price of about $0.95 is between 1.9 and 2.4 percent of the capture line, and the full published retouch band runs from about 0.5 to about 3.1 percent of it. Retouch is not production.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: two published pricing categories, both collected September 2026, divided one into the other. Capture from two studio cards, retouch from two editing cards, all four read directly.</p>

  <p>Around two percent. When someone quotes you a per-image figure that sounds impossibly low, check which of those two things they are selling, because the answer is almost always the cheaper one.</p>

  <p>Retouch has its own volume floor, and it is steeper than photography's.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">One published retouch card guarantees 200 edited images a day with next-morning delivery, and declines anyone under 1,000 images a year, sending them to a trial instead. Its $95 monthly platform fee doubles the unit price at 100 images a month and adds about 10 percent at 1,000.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published image editing pricing page in this category, read 10 September 2026. The access condition and the fee are stated on the card, the amortization is division.</p>

  <p>A supplier that turns away small buyers is telling you its economics only work above a threshold. At 100 images a month that platform fee doubles what you pay per image, which makes the headline rate meaningless at your volume.</p>

  <h2>When is generation cheaper than shooting?</h2>

  <p>At the point where the ladder stops helping, which the curve above puts at around 500 images a year.</p>

  <p>Be careful with the reasoning here, because this is where cost pages usually overclaim. What the published evidence supports is narrow: capture prices stop falling with volume, while the number of images you need does not stop rising. Anything that reduces the count of images requiring a camera therefore attacks the only variable still moving.</p>

  <p>hubStudio publishes a mix figure for its own delivery, and it is worth reading for what it is.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio publishes that roughly 70 percent of its delivered imagery is generated and 30 percent captured. That is a published delivery mix on hubStudio's own pages, not a market ratio and not a measurement with a stated sample or period.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio site copy, September 2026, attributed to the page that publishes it.</p>

  <p>No claim is made here about where that ratio moves with catalog size, because no sample and no period sit behind it. It is one studio's published mix, offered so you know the shape of the thing being described rather than as evidence for a decision.</p>

  <p>The decision itself is more usefully made per asset than per catalog.</p>

