  <p>The pattern in that table is that capture makes the master and generation multiplies it. That is a production sequence rather than a choice between two suppliers, and it is why the useful question is what share of your images need a camera, not which vendor is cheaper per image.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">ecommerce design service</a> page covers how a catalog shoot is actually run, the <a href="/solutions/retailers">retailers solutions page</a> covers the catalog end of it, and the <a href="/resources/production-cost">production cost estimator</a> will take your own volume and mix and return a market range rather than a quote.</p>

  <h2>How this page was built</h2>

  <p>Every band was read directly from a published price surface in September 2026, one card at a time, and is quoted against the unit that card states. Where a figure is derived, the arithmetic is shown on the page and the inputs are named. Bands built from two or more independent cards are given as ranges. A band drawn from a single card is labeled as such. Nothing here comes from an aggregator, a roundup or a vendor's estimate of somebody else's prices.</p>

  <p>Two limits worth stating. The curve above is one card's published ladder carried across volumes, not an average of the market, and it is labeled that way wherever it appears. And there is no independent benchmark for any of this: across the buyer phrasings searched, every page returned that published a price was published by someone selling the service. That is not a reason to distrust the cards, which are primary evidence of their own prices. It is a reason to distrust any page claiming to know the market average, including one that sounded confident.</p>

  <p>Reviewed September 2026.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about per-SKU photography cost</h2>

  <h3>How much does product photography cost per SKU?</h3>

  <p>Published studio cards put standard ecommerce capture at roughly $39 to $50 an image in September 2026, with uplifts to about $190 for full-body model images. Per SKU depends on how many images each SKU needs, and no source publishes an average for that, because it is a decision about your catalog rather than a market fact.</p>

  <h3>Does cost per SKU fall at volume?</h3>

  <p>Up to a point, then it flattens hard. Published ladders cap at about 30 percent off list in both markets read, with the deepest tier reached around 500 images a year. Carried across volumes, going from 12,000 to 30,000 images moves effective capture from about $35.63 to about $35.25, roughly 1 percent.</p>

  <h3>What does a photography day rate not cover?</h3>

  <p>Sample logistics, reshoots and approval rounds beyond the first. Eight published price surfaces were read across two markets and not one prices any of the three. Fixed booking fees also hide inside day-rate framing: one card's $149 booking fee is 62 percent of the unit price at six images from a booking and under 1 percent at six hundred.</p>

  <h3>When is generation cheaper than shooting?</h3>

  <p>Once the published discount ladder stops helping, which is around 500 images a year. Past that, the rate is nearly fixed and the only variable left is how many images need a camera at all. Decide it per asset: capture makes the master where material accuracy matters, generation multiplies it into variants.</p>

  <h3>How much does ecommerce retouching cost per image?</h3>

  <p>Published retouch cards run roughly $0.25 to $1.20 an image by operation, which is about 0.5 to 3.1 percent of the published capture line. One card adds a fixed platform fee of about $95 a month, which doubles the effective unit price at 100 images a month and adds about 10 percent at 1,000.</p>
